\DOC WITNESS_F_EVENTS_TAC

\TYPE {WITNESS_F_EVENTS_TAC : thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Instantiates the leading existential of the goal with a witness function built
from a supplied theorem, leaving one subgoal in which the existential variable
is replaced by an explicit lambda-abstraction applied to the original
arguments.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {th_noexists : thm} is the theorem from which the witness term for
the existentially quantified function is constructed. Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is {?f. t}, the tactic strips the outer existential and returns a
single subgoal whose conclusion is {t} with {f} replaced by a beta-unreduced
lambda term {\x1 ... xn. ...} derived from {th_noexists}; consequently the
applications of {f} in the body appear as that abstraction applied to the
original argument terms, with one argument position adjusted by an offset.
Everything else in the conclusion — the surrounding universal quantifiers,
implications and other structure — is unchanged, and the assumptions are
untouched.
{
   A ?- ?f. t
   ================================  WITNESS_F_EVENTS_TAC th_noexists
   A ?- t[(\x1 ... xn. ...) / f]
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this one kind of outcome occurs, always on goals
with no assumptions whose conclusion is a single existential over a function
used in event-list equations.

\ENDDOC
